WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.180
- [Narrator] Churros, the
delectable deep fried pastry.

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:06.030
Gooey and soft on the inside
with ridges on the outside

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:08.270
that gather pockets of cinnamon and sugar

00:00:08.270 --> 00:00:10.610
and help give it that classic crunch.

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:12.360
Today, we'll show you how to make Tasty's

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.194
ultimate churro recipe.

00:00:14.194 --> 00:00:16.861
(upbeat music)

00:00:18.542 --> 00:00:19.910
So who created the churros, anyway?

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:21.010
Great question.

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:22.080
We don't know.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.530
The origin of the churro
is heavily debated.

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:26.740
Some say it originated
from Spanish shepherds

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:28.680
as an on-the-go replacement for bread.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.480
It's said that the iconic curled shape

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:32.480
was made to resemble
that of a sheep's horn.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:35.360
But another recipe was
discovered in ancient Rome

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.370
for a flour and water fritter

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:38.860
that had strong similarities

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:40.470
to the modern-day churro as well.

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.750
There's even a theory
that Portuguese sailors

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:45.220
brought back youtiao, a similar pastry

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:47.950
from China to Portugal
and spread into Spain.

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:49.390
Though, we can't say for sure

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:51.760
where the first variation
of the churro originated.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:53.940
We can tell you that today,
we're going to be making

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:56.090
our favorite recipe for churros.

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:58.070
Regardless of its past,

00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:01.350
Every churro starts with a
pate a choux or a choux pastry,

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:03.780
which is just a mixture of flour, eggs,

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:06.810
butter, sugar, salt, milk, and water.

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:08.740
It's the same dough used for desserts

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:10.560
like cream puffs and eclairs.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.850
We'll start with our wet ingredients.

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:15.550
We tested a few different
water and milk ratios

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:18.320
and found that the half
milk, half water ratio

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:20.520
helps the dough retain the
right amount of moisture

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:21.870
while giving it the best flavor.

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:23.610
We'll add our liquids to a sauce pan,

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:27.270
along with some sugar, salt,
butter, and vanilla bean.

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:30.030
You can use vanilla extract if
that's what you have on hand,

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:32.290
but since we're making
the ultimate churros,

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:35.300
we want to pack in as much
vanilla flavor as possible.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:38.170
It'll also give us a
beautiful speckled dough.

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:39.570
Once that comes to a boil,

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:42.270
we'll remove the vanilla bean
and then add in the flour.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:44.070
Reduce the heat to medium low,

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:45.520
and stir with a wooden spoon

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:46.960
until the mixture forms a ball

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:49.790
and no longer sticks to
the pan, about 5 minutes.

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:51.300
You'll know the flour is cooked

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.230
when it starts to pull away from the pan,

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:55.360
leaving no residue on the sides or bottom.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:57.480
This should take about five minutes.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:58.730
Now for the eggs.

00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:00.950
We tested a variety of egg combinations,

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:02.980
and we found that two eggs made the inside

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:04.410
of the churro too gummy.

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:07.100
But four eggs made it too airy and spongy.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:09.140
So three whole eggs was the winner.

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:12.150
Not too fluffy, not too dense, just right.

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:13.780
We'll transfer our dough to a bowl

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:16.250
and let it cool for about 5 minutes.

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:17.730
If it's too hot during this step,

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:20.210
you might end up with bits of
scrambled eggs in your dough,

00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:22.410
and we definitely don't want that.

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:24.540
Let's talk about mixing utensils.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:27.420
We found that using a stand
mixer with a paddle attachment

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.380
is the best way to mix the dough.

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:30.860
If you don't have a stand mixer,

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:32.880
using a wooden spoon works great.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.200
It'll keep the dough at
a constant temperature.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:36.540
It's just a little more work,

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:40.380
and we here at Tasty are
all about simplicity.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:42.650
A metal whisk or an electric hand mixer

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:44.640
will transfer too much heat to the dough,

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.290
or it could whip too
much air into your dough.

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:48.580
Egg-cellent.

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:50.200
Now that our dough is cooled down,

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:51.820
let's add the eggs one at a time,

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:54.100
making sure to completely mix one egg in

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:55.360
before adding the next.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:57.600
It's going to look really
separated and broken,

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.060
but it'll come together.

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:01.100
The finished dough should
be gracefully falling

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:03.180
from the paddle, it shouldn't plop off.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.310
It should look like a thick custard.

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:06.800
Let's move on to piping.

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:09.000
There are two main ways
to pipe your churros.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.260
You can pipe them by hand using
a piping bag with a star tip

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:13.950
or use a churro maker.

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:16.440
We tested both ways and
found that the churro maker

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.480
didn't make a huge difference
in ease or efficiency,

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:21.100
but it does change the shape.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.850
So if you have a churro maker
on hand, feel free to use it.

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:26.340
But hand piping is totally fine too.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:29.020
We'll be using a star
tip to pipe our churros.

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:30.800
Not only is it the most traditional,

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:33.930
but the iconic star shape
increases the surface area,

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:35.540
which allows for a bigger crunch

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:38.240
and also makes for a sturdier churro.

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:41.660
Pro tip, let the dough rest
for five minutes before piping.

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:43.350
this will allow the
dough to set up a little

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:45.010
and make piping easier.

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:48.110
We also experimented with a
few other methods of resting.

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:50.100
Chilling and freezing.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:52.570
The frozen churros brought the
temperature of the oil down

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:54.070
and didn't get as crispy,

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:56.560
plus you run the risk of
browning on the outside

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:58.210
without cooking it all the way through.

00:03:58.210 --> 00:03:59.520
While chilling in the fridge

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:01.970
made the dough too sticky
to get off the plate.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:03.420
We achieve the best results

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:05.410
piping it straight into the oil.

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:08.760
Now it wouldn't be churros
without a sweet coating.

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.160
You can go crazy with
your churro toppings,

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:13.300
but for recipe, we have decided to stick

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:15.810
to the traditional cinnamon sugar coating.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:17.490
We're using ceylon cinnamon.

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:20.810
Ceylon cinnamon is brighter
and has more of a floral taste.

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:23.850
While cassia is more common
and a little more subtle.

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:26.800
A good rule of thumb is to
add one teaspoon of cinnamon

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:28.550
to every half cup of sugar.

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:31.200
We're using good old
granulated sugar here.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:33.770
This small granules stick
to the churros nicely

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:35.190
and fit better into all of those

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:37.010
delicious nooks and crannies.

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:38.670
Now it's time to cook our churros.

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:40.650
Our method of choice is deep frying.

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:42.560
We found that it's the
only way to really get

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:46.010
that classic, crispy
exterior and soft center.

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:47.580
You just won't get the same results

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:49.580
in an air fryer or the oven.

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:51.370
The best temperature to fry your churros

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:53.640
is 350 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:56.000
This will ensure your churros
are fully cooked through

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:58.360
with the perfect crispy outside.

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:00.710
Frying at too high of a
temperature could again,

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:03.650
leave you with a burnt
outside and a raw center.

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:05.160
You can totally stop here

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:07.150
and enjoy your well-deserved churros.

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:08.780
But we're going to amp
things up a little bit

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:11.070
with a spicy chocolate dipping sauce.

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:12.760
We're going to start with
the base of the sauce,

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:14.660
which is our classic ganache.

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:16.180
First we'll heat up our cream

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:18.560
and then add in our cayenne and cinnamon.

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:20.490
We're using semi-sweet chocolate chips.

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:22.190
So our dip has a little bitterness,

00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:23.430
which will blend really well

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:25.513
with our sweet cinnamon sugar coating.

00:05:26.420 --> 00:05:30.160
Now for the sweetest
part, serving them up.

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:31.970
Pairing our golden hot churros

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:33.960
with our spicy chocolate dipping sauce

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:36.260
makes for the ultimate combo.

00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:40.163
The perfect crispy outside
and soft pillowy inside.

00:05:40.163 --> 00:05:41.140
Churr-ay!

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:43.160
Our churros are finally ready to eat,

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:45.260
so let's give them a taste.

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:49.030
Yum, one bite of these
churros will have you saying

00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:50.193
- [Multiple People] Oh yes!

00:05:52.203 --> 00:05:54.786
(light music)

